  "content": {
    "id": "p5-q8-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "8",
    "prompt": "Show that $\\triangle PQR$ is a right-angled triangle where the coordinates of $P$, $Q$ and $R$ are $(3,3)$, $(3,-1)$ and $(6,3)$, respectively.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$PQ\\perp PR$, so $\\angle QPR=90^\\circ$.\n\nThe squared side lengths satisfy $PQ^2+PR^2=QR^2$.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}PQ^2&=(3-3)^2+(-1-3)^2\\\\PQ^2&=16\\\\PR^2&=(6-3)^2+(3-3)^2\\\\PR^2&=9\\\\QR^2&=(6-3)^2+(3-(-1))^2\\\\QR^2&=25\\\\PQ^2+PR^2&=16+9\\\\PQ^2+PR^2&=25=QR^2\\end{align*}$$\nBy the converse of Pythagoras’ theorem, $\\triangle PQR$ is right-angled at $P$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 58
  },
